# Secrets / environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# GitLab Runner registration material. These appear in the repository root the
# moment an operator registers a runner, and they are NOT ignored by default.
#
# `config.toml` holds the runner authentication token IN PLAINTEXT. It is untracked,
# but "untracked" is not a control -- one `git add -A` commits a credential that
# authenticates every job on the runner. This is the same failure the repository's
# own policy guard refuses on the write side, arriving through a different door.
#
# `.runner_system_id` is the runner's identity; harmless alone, but it is part of
# the same registration pair and is ignored for the same reason.
config.toml
.runner_system_id

# Dependencies
node_modules/
venv/
.venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Logs / runtime data
*.log
/logs/
workspace/*
!workspace/.gitkeep

# Credentials vault (D-016). Covered by the wildcard above; named explicitly so
# the intent is readable without tracing the pattern. The vault file is the one
# place values are stored unredacted, so it must never reach the repository.
workspace/vault/

# OS / IDE
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# Test / coverage
coverage/
.nyc_output/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Temporary files
*.tmp
*.temp
*.swp

!tests/fixtures/**/*.log
.session-archive/
# Root-anchored: without the leading slash this also matched
# platform/omniroute/, because git match-ignores case on Windows. That silently
# dropped the entire Omniroute gateway from every commit that added it.
/OmniRoute/
.pi-tools/

# Spent probe/epitaph files. These were kept because the deletion guard refused
# their removal, and docs/POLICY_PORT.md is explicit that the correct response to
# a refused deletion is to leave the file, not work around the guard. They are
# kept as history and are deliberately NOT part of the test run: a dot-prefixed
# `*.test.ts` under platform/pi is inert to the runner's glob, and leaving them
# tracked would put a dead test-shaped file in the suite's blast radius.
platform/pi/.c1-probe.test.ts
platform/pi/.s8-probe.test.ts

# Verification scratch. `*.tmp` does not match these: the suffix is `-patch.txt`
# on a dot-prefixed basename, which is exactly the shape the old rule missed.
verification/.tmp-*.txt
verification/.tmp-*

# Agent scratchpad for throwaway probes and one-shot patch scripts. Never a
# deliverable: anything that matters is moved into platform/ and committed.
.scratch/

# Local vitest transcripts. A full run prints thousands of TAP lines and piping
# one to a file is the natural thing to do while debugging -- which left
# vitest-out.txt, vitest-out3.txt and vitest-full.txt sitting in the repository
# root as untracked noise. A paste is better than a file, and a file that
# outlives the bug it recorded is worse than no file at all.
vitest-*.txt
# Ad-hoc build output from a frontend run. Same reasoning: nobody reads it twice.
builds/

# THE OPERATOR'S BOOT SCRIPTS. Ignored, not deleted.
#
# `boot.js` and `boot.mjs` are the wedge bootstrap the operator runs by hand. They
# carry a CREDENTIAL, which is the whole reason they were untracked and still
# dangerous: untracked and unignored means the next `git add -A` walks them into a
# commit, and a credential in history is a credential to rotate.
#
# **Gitignored rather than removed**, and the distinction is deliberate. A file
# that is configuration carrying a value should be reconstructed if it is ever
# lost -- removing it destroys work to prevent a mistake an ignore rule prevents
# outright, at no cost. An ignore rule says "never commit this"; a deletion says
# "this never existed", and only the operator can say which of those two they
# meant.
boot.js
boot.mjs

# One-shot patch scripts written beside the thing they patch. Three of them
# (patch-install-tmp.mjs, prune-tmp.mjs, styles-install-tmp.mjs) were committed
# by an  that did not mean to include them -- which is the failure
# this rule exists to make quieter. They are throwaway by construction: whatever
# they do belongs in a commit, and the script outliving it is a file nobody
# maintains. They stay ON DISK; they are simply never tracked.
frontend/*-tmp.mjs

data/

# The wedge launcher workspace: the owner drops CSVs in here and reports are
# written beside them. Their files, not ours, so never a commit.
ui-workspace/

# Generated by `npm run archify` (depcruise). A ~200KB artefact that changes on
# every dependency edit, so the CONFIG is tracked and the OUTPUT is not.
architecture.html

# An external skill artefact, present on this machine before this work began.
# Not ours to track, and not part of the kernel's source: a vendored bundle
# would drag its own changelog and node_modules into every review that touches
# a file list.
skills/ui-ux-pro-max-skill/


.env
gate.db
gate.db-journal
hive/
roster.json
roster-backups/
*.js
*.orig.ts
debug-*/
